---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | CCA Web Archive Gallery"
  heading="About the CCA Web Archive"
  navHref="../"
  navLabel="Archive"
  navAriaLabel="Return to archive gallery"
>
  <article class="about-page">
    <p class="lede">
      This site collects archives of institutional websites from California
      College of the Arts. It is a memorial to the college's web presence, most
      of which will be taken offline when the college closes in June, 2027.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use the Web Archive</h2>
    <p>
      You can search through the archived sites by title, domain, and
      description. The search does not cover <em>site content</em>. You can also
      filter sites by how they were archived. Sites were archived using three
      different means:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Static <abbr title="Hypertext Markup Language">HTML</abbr></strong> - The site was mirrored to static HTML files.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Internet Archive</strong> - The site was archived by the Internet
        Archive and is available for playback in the <a
          href="https://web.archive.org/">Wayback Machine</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Webrecorder</strong> - The site was archived using Webrecorder for dynamic playback.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Static sites can be navigated <em>internally</em> as though they were
      online. Links to external sites may break. Note that links <em
        >between archived sites</em
      > won't work either but you can check this archive to see if we have a copy
      of the site being linked to.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Internet Archive, on the other hand, can mostly be navigated as if a
      site was live. You can follow internal and external links, which will work if an
      archived copy exists. Note that some media may be missing or unplayable.
      If you land on a media asset via navigation which the Internet Archive claims isn't available, you can
      sometimes access it by feeding its <abbr title="Uniform Resource Locator">URL</abbr> back
      into the Wayback Machine as a
      starting point.
    </p>

    <p>
      Embedded media on some of the Static HTML sites will only work as long as
      the externally hosted resources are online. For instance, videos hosted on
      CCA's Panopto platform will cease functioning when CCA closes and Panopto
      is shut down. Some sites (e.g. "Make. Act. Resist.") already have broken
      embeds to videos that were deaccessioned.
    </p>

    <h2>Team</h2>
    <ul class="team-list">
      <li>
        Eric Phetteplace, Systems Librarian. Site design, web archiving, project
        management.
      </li>
      <li>Mark Goh, Site Reliability Engineer. Inventory of CCA websites.</li>
      <li>
        Nancy Chan, Instructional Technologist. Quality assurance and web
        archiving.
      </li>
      <li>Tanza Solis, Web Developer. Site design and web archiving.</li>
      <li>Joel Lithgow, Web Developer. Site design and digital archiving.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Technical Details</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/cca/cca-web-archives"
        >cca/cca-web-archives</a
      > repository on GitHub contains some notes and scripts in <a
        href="https://github.com/cca/cca-web-archives/tree/main/docs"
        >the docs folder</a
      > which were used in this project. We compiled a list of existing CCA sites
      from a server inventory spreadsheet and <abbr title="Domain Name System">DNS</abbr> records.
      Our inventory undoubtedly missed some sites, especially official ones run by
      academic programs or student organizations but not using CCA infrastructure.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of the Static HTML sites were mirrored to HTML files
      using <code>wget</code> then hosted on GitHub Pages.
      This approach was appropriate for smaller sites
      which do not rely on <abbr title="Application Programming Interface">API</abbr>s or other dynamic content, such as "<a
        href="https://cca.github.io/makeactresist.cca.edu/index.html"
        >Make. Act. Resist.</a
      >" and <a href="https://cca.github.io/visualnarratives.cca.edu/index.html"
        >Visual Narratives (Spring 2021)</a
      >. Some of the modern sites like the <a
        href="https://cca.github.io/cca-2026-celebration-site/"
        >2026 Commencement Celebration</a
      > and <a href="https://cca.github.io/after-intelligence-cca-edu/"
        >After Intelligence</a
      > already had their code hosted on GitHub and were able to be deployed to GitHub
      Pages using a custom workflow. These were designed by Joel Lithgow for Marketing
      & Communications and use the <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> framework.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many CCA websites, especially our more popular ones, already had coverage
      in in <a href="https://archive.org/">the Internet Archive</a>. We used the <a
        href="https://github.com/edgi-govdata-archiving/wayback">wayback</a
      > python library and code contained in the "<a
        href="https://github.com/cca/cca-web-archives/tree/main/ia">ia</a
      >" folder of the cca-web-archives project to programmatically investigate
      the coverage of larger CCA sites. Complete sets of URLs from some web
      properties, such as <a
        href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260223175036/https://libraries.cca.edu/"
        >the CCA Libraries</a
      > and the <a
        href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260722190046/https://www.cca.edu/exhibitions/rewind-review-respond/"
        >Rewind Review Respond</a
      > publication, were submitted to the Internet Archive using their <a
        href="https://archive.org/services/wayback-gsheets/"
        >Save Page Now for Google Sheets</a
      > service. CCA also reached out to the Internet Archive for assistance with
      archiving our entire web presence. The <a
        href="https://carta.archive-it.org/about/"
        >Collaborative ART Archive (CARTA)</a
      > graciously agreed to archive our sites. The representation of CCA websites
      as they existed in 2026 should be fairly complete in the Internet Archive.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://webrecorder.net/">Webrecorder</a> was used for smaller dynamic
      sites that we could manually navigate through in a reasonable amount of time
      but were unable to be captured successfully with <code>wget</code>, such as <a
        href="https://cca.github.io/build.cca.edu/">BuildLab</a
      >. We used the Webrecorder Chrome Extension to create the archive and
      hosted <a
        href="https://github.com/cca/build.cca.edu/blob/main/build.cca.edu.wacz"
      >
        the WACZ file
      </a> used for playback on GitHub using <a href="https://git-lfs.com/"
        >Git LFS</a
      >.
    </p>
  </article>

  <style>
    .about-page {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 3rem auto 4rem;
    }

    .about-page .lede {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: #555;
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .about-page h2 {
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
    }

    .about-page p,
    .about-page li {
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .team-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .team-list li {
      margin: 0.4rem 0;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
